<h2>China's scale in the Indian shrimp trade</h2>
<p>China took 136,164 MT of Indian frozen shrimp in FY2024-25 — the second-largest single-country market after the United States, and well ahead of the European Union's 99,310 MT, confirmed directly from MPEDA's own official press release. In value terms, China was also the largest seafood export destination from India by quantity across all seafood categories that year: 396,424 MT worth USD 1,276.58 million. This isn't a niche or emerging relationship; it's one of the two anchor markets that the Indian shrimp export industry is built around.</p>

<h2>GACC/CIFER registration comes before anything else</h2>
<p>If there's one thing to sort out before you commit to a supplier or a timeline, it's this. China's Decree 248 requires facility-level registration with the General Administration of Customs of China (GACC), recorded in the CIFER (China Import Food Enterprises Registration) system, before many food categories, seafood included, can clear Chinese customs. This registration applies to the specific processing facility, and it's a process with real lead time — not something that can be resolved during your first shipment's transit. GACC issued Decree No. 280 in October 2025, which will replace Decree 248 from 1 June 2026; the registration requirement itself isn't going away, so this is a timing detail to be aware of rather than a reason to wait.</p>
<p>If you're working with us for the first time on China-bound shrimp, confirm registration status at the enquiry stage. It's a simple question with a potentially long answer if it hasn't been done yet.</p>

<h2>Don't assume another market's compliance transfers</h2>
<p>This trips up buyers who source from the same supplier for multiple destinations. China sets its own residue and quality standards independently — they aren't harmonised with EU or US limits, and product tested and cleared for one market isn't automatically compliant for China. Tell us specifically that China is your destination, and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese standard rather than assuming a US or EU clearance carries over.</p>

<h2>Form and specification</h2>
<p>As with any shrimp order, specify form (HOSO, HLSO, PD, PUD, PDTO or cooked), count size, and whether you need IQF or block-frozen. Chinese buyers span the full range of use cases — some are importing for further domestic processing (favoring HOSO or HLSO), others for direct retail or food service (often PD or cooked). Tell us your intended use and we'll recommend the right form rather than defaulting to whatever's easiest to quote.</p>

<h2>Logistics and timing</h2>
<p>Transit runs 15-22 days to major Chinese ports (Shanghai, Shenzhen/Yantian, Qingdao) — mid-range compared with our other lanes, shorter than the US route and longer than Southeast Asia. Plan your registration and documentation lead time separately from shipping transit; the GACC/CIFER process is usually the longer pole in the timeline for a first order.</p>

